{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UtcbkdjaZMoazkF51fwH8f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current State**:\n- All PRDs are written (prd001 through prd010)\n- All use cases and test suites are complete\n- No code has been implemented yet\n- Currently in the specification phase\n\n**Roadmap**:\n- rel01.0 (in_progress): ts - PRD complete, use case written, test suite written, implementation pending\n- rel01.1 (pending): wc, cat, sponge\n- rel02.0 (pending): ls core, du\n- rel02.1 (pending)\n\nThe first incomplete release is rel01.0 with the ts utility. Since pkg/testutils is a dependency for all command implementations, I need to build that first before tackling cmd/ts. Given the constraint of creating only one task, the priority is implementing pkg/testutils as the foundational library.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the specific requirements for the testutils package—it needs to handle test case structures, binary execution with timeouts and working directories, output comparison with normalization hooks, and file-state verification for utilities that write to files rather than stdout.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria, the main API is a RunDiffTests function that takes a test instance, two binaries, and test cases, with passing tests producing silent output and failures printing details. I'm estimating around 250 lines total across the struct definitions, the main runner function, binary execution helpers, comparison logic, timestamp normalization, and file comparison—which fits the target scope.\n\nFollowing the naming convention, this should be `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` with a corresponding test file. Now I need to map the requirements carefully: the test data structure maps to R1, the runner function to R2, the normalizers to R3, file expectations to R4, and timeout handling to R5, which gives me a solid foundation for the task description. I'm pulling the concrete design decisions directly from the PRD now—the configurable timeout with a 10-second default, using t.Run for subtests, composable normalizer functions, and the RunDiffTests API signature. Given the 250-350 line constraint and the preference to keep this as a single task, I'll include both implementation and tests together rather than splitting them.\n\nSince tests don't count toward the production code budget, I can write the full harness implementation plus comprehensive unit tests without exceeding the limit. The task title needs to anchor to specific PRD requirement IDs, so I'm going with something like \"pkg/testutils: Implement DiffTest harness (prd001 R1-R5).\" Now I'm drafting the full task description with clear acceptance criteria tied back to those requirements.\n\nI'm confirming this is a greenfield implementation—the project context shows everything is pending and no code exists yet. For the YAML output, I need to be careful with the literal block scalar formatting since the description itself contains YAML. The stitch agent will need the full PRD, architecture documentation, and module path to understand the conventions. I'm planning three files: the main DiffTest implementation with its core functions and helpers, a normalizers module for timestamp handling and composition, and a diff utilities file.\n\nActually, I should reconsider the file structure—the normalizers are tightly coupled to DiffTest and the total code is only around 250 lines, so keeping everything in one file might be cleaner. Per the naming convention, the primary file should be named after the primary exported type, which is DiffTest, so difftest.go is the right choice. I'll consolidate the normalizers there since they're part of the same public API.\n\nNow I'm working through the task description, mapping out the requirements and acceptance criteria to give the stitch agent clear direction without any ambiguity. 1-R2.5\nR3: Implement comparison and divergence reporting per prd001 R3.1-R3.6\nR4: Implement normalization hooks including TimestampNormalizer per prd001 R4.1-R4.3\nR5: Implement file-state comparison per prd001 R5.1-R5.2\n\nDesign decisions:\nD1: Primary file is difftest.go after the primary exported type DiffTest (per project convention: package file is named after the primary exported type, not the package name)\nD2: Use os/exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout for binary invocation; default timeout 10 seconds per prd001 R2.3\nD3: RunDiffTests calls t.Run with the test name for each DiffTest to produce named subtests per prd001 R3.6\nD4: Normalizers chain together through a ComposeNormalizers helper that applies a slice of them in sequence per prd001 R4.3\n\nAcceptance criteria:\nAC1: A caller can define []DiffTest and call RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests) with no other setup per prd001 AC1\nAC2: A passing test run produces no output; a failing test prints args, stdin, both stdout/stderr, and both exit codes per prd001 AC2\nAC3: TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps with a fixed placeholder per prd001 AC3\nAC4: A DiffTest with ExpectedFiles correctly fails when file contents diverge per prd001 AC4\nAC5: A DiffTest that exceeds the timeout fails with a clear timeout message per prd001 AC5\nAC6: All code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint per prd001 AC6\nAC7: Unit tests in difftest_test.go verify comparison logic, normalization, and timeout behavior\n\nI'm confirming the scope is right—7 acceptance criteria fits within the 5-8 range for code tasks, and I have 5 requirements and 4 design decisions which also align with the guidelines. Now I need to finalize the required_reading section with the actual file paths the stitch agent can access.\n\nThe PRD is at `docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml`, the architecture lives in `docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml`, and I should reference `go.mod` for the module path. Since this is greenfield work, there's no existing code to read, but the agent needs to understand the module structure at github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils. The acceptance criteria are already documented in the PRD itself, so I don't need to pull in a separate test suite file.\n\nNow I'm being more explicit about what the stitch agent needs to implement. I'm laying out the exact struct fields and function signatures from the PRD so there's no ambiguity—DiffTest needs Name, Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, Normalize, WorkDir, and ExpectedFiles with their specific types, and Normalize is a function type that transforms byte slices. I'm also specifying the RunDiffTests function signature and making sure the requirements capture all the implementation details needed.\n\nFor execution, I'm requiring context-based timeouts with a 10-second default, independent capture of stdout/stderr/exit codes from both binaries, and clear timeout error messages. The comparison logic applies normalizers in sequence to both outputs, then does byte-for-byte comparison of normalized stdout and stderr plus exact exit code matching. When tests fail, the error reporting includes the test args, truncated stdin, and the full output diff between reference and Go binary.\n\nNow I need to add normalizer utilities—a TimestampNormalizer that strips out common timestamp formats like strftime patterns and ISO-8601 datetimes, plus a ComposeNormalizers function to chain multiple normalizers together. Then for file comparison, if ExpectedFiles is populated, I'll validate each file's content byte-for-byte after both binaries finish and report any divergences the same way as output mismatches.\n\nActually, wait—I'm conflating the requirements. The PRD specifies exactly 5 requirement groups, so my task requirements should map 1:1 to those. Let me refocus: R1 covers the DiffTest struct and Normalize function type with all the fields I need, R2 handles the actual binary execution, and I should keep the remaining requirements aligned with the PRD structure rather than splitting them further. (R2.1-R2.5). Execute goBinary and refBinary with identical Args, Stdin, and Env. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code independently. Use configurable timeout (default 10 seconds) via context.WithTimeout. Support WorkDir field; when empty, create a per-test temp dir. Produce no output on passing tests.\n\nR3: Implement comparison and divergence reporting per prd001-testutils R3 (R3.1-R3.6). Apply Normalize functions (if non-nil) to both outputs before comparison. Compare normalized stdout, stderr byte-for-byte and exit codes exactly. On divergence, report: test Args, Stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), reference stdout/stderr/exit code, Go binary stdout/stderr/exit code. Run each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...).\n\nR4: Build normalization hooks per prd001-testutils R4 (R4.1-R4.3). Support per-DiffTest normalization via the Normalize slice. Provide TimestampNormalizer that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed placeholder string \"TIMESTAMP\". Provide ComposeNormalizers helper that chains a slice of Normalize functions applied in order.\n\nR5: Implement file-state comparison per prd001-testutils R5 (R5.1-R5.2). When ExpectedFiles is non-nil, compare expected file content byte-for-byte after both binaries exit. Report file content divergence in the same format as stdout divergence.\n\nAll five requirements map directly to the PRD requirement groups. Now I'm finalizing the full output and being careful about YAML formatting for the description field.\n\nI need to think through what the test file should actually test. The harness runs external binaries, so the tests need dummy binaries to compare against—I could use small compiled Go programs as test helpers, or rely on system utilities like `echo` and `cat` for simplicity. The tests should cover identical and different outputs, timestamp normalization with known patterns, timeout behavior, and file-state comparison.\n\nI should also note that `go.mod` already exists at the root with the module `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils`, so the stitch agent will have that context. The project structure shows `pkg/testutils/` as a role, meaning these are new files being created. I'll finalize this as a single task without over-prescribing the testing approach—the agent can decide between test helpers or system utilities.\n\nFor the task title, I need to include the requirement ID: \"pkg/testutils: Implement DiffTest harness (prd001 R1-R5)\". Now I'm mapping out the files section with precise paths and actions: `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` will contain the DiffTest struct, Normalize type, RunDiffTests function, and the binary execution and comparison logic. I'm being careful not to invent design decisions beyond what the PRD and architecture specify, and I'm skipping anything like `doc.go` that isn't mentioned in the requirements. I'm structuring the YAML output with the full deliverable details—the required reading includes the PRD, architecture docs, and module path, while the files section specifies creating the DiffTest harness, normalizers, and test utilities with their specific responsibilities.\n\nNow I'm defining the core requirements, starting with the DiffTest struct fields like Name, Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, and a slice of Normalize functions to apply during comparison.\n\nI'm also laying out the RunDiffTests API that will execute both binaries with identical inputs, capture their outputs independently, and enforce a configurable timeout with a 10-second default. failure. On divergence, report in the failure message: the DiffTest Args, the Stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, the reference stderr, the Go stderr, and both exit codes. Run each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...) so individual test case failures are identifiable in go test output.\n\nFor normalization, I need to support per-DiffTest normalization through a Normalize slice field, and provide a built-in TimestampNormalizer function that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed \"TIMESTAMP\" placeholder.\n\nI'm handling three timestamp formats: syslog format with three-letter month and space-padded day, ISO-8601 datetime with optional fractional seconds and timezone, and HH:MM:SS elapsed time. I'll use regexp.ReplaceAll for the pattern matching, and create a ComposeNormalizers helper that chains multiple normalization functions together, returning the input unchanged when the slice is empty.\n\nNext I'm implementing file-state comparison where DiffTest.ExpectedFiles gets checked. Normalizers are kept separate to maintain focus on the core execution logic, with timestamp normalization using precompiled regex patterns for efficiency. The acceptance criteria specify that tests should integrate seamlessly into any cmd/ package, produce silent passes and detailed failure output including truncated stdin and both binaries' outputs, and handle timestamp normalization by replacing specific patterns. Verifying that the unit tests cover all the key scenarios—identical outputs passing, divergent stdout/stderr/exit codes failing, timestamp normalization working correctly, normalizer chaining functioning as expected, and timeout handling working properly. I should also double-check the YAML structure to make sure the literal block scalars are valid and that I'm using the right dash characters throughout. I'm checking the indentation handling in nested YAML literal blocks—when the outer description uses `|`, the inner content preserves its structure correctly when parsed as YAML. Now I need to verify that all the field names in my schema match what the constitution requires: deliverable_type, required_reading, files with their specific mappings, and requirements.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71597,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71597},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cfc4efcc-a511-46b2-8f82-2c74fbf1012c","uuid":"5ee78cea-9967-4e50-8d18-c3821aa210e8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UtcbkdjaZMoazkF51fwH8f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at the project context, I need to analyze the current state and determine the next task.\n\n**Project State Summary:**\n- go-unix-utils is a specification-driven pipeline: extract specs from C source, generate Go implementations, verify via differential testing against GNU reference binaries\n- All PRDs (prd001-prd010), use cases, and test suites are written — the specification phase is largely complete\n- No implementation code exists yet\n- Current release: rel01.0 (in_progress) — ts utility, one use case remaining\n\n**Priority Analysis:**\n- rel01.0 requires: pkg/testutils (differential testing harness) + cmd/ts (the utility)\n- pkg/testutils is a dependency for ALL utilities — it must be built first\n- The single task should be: implement pkg/testutils per prd001\n\nLet me write the task to the output file."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71597,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71597},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cfc4efcc-a511-46b2-8f82-2c74fbf1012c","uuid":"e50a43c1-4259-4b5f-85e1-9acd6d9e3f80"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UtcbkdjaZMoazkF51fwH8f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012opjxiyZ135oKSJfDmd332","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/petardjukic/2025-objectives/go-unix-utils/.cobbler/measure-20260224-215424.yaml","content":"- index: 0\n  title: \"pkg/testutils: Implement DiffTest harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (full PRD for pkg/testutils -- defines DiffTest struct, binary execution, comparison, normalization, and file-state requirements)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (component descriptions for pkg/testutils, project structure, technology choices)\n      - go.mod (Go module path: github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, Normalize type, RunDiffTests function, binary execution and comparison logic\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalizers.go\n        action: create\n        note: TimestampNormalizer and ComposeNormalizers helper\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for RunDiffTests, comparison, normalization, timeout, and file-state comparison\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Define DiffTest struct and Normalize function type per prd001-testutils R1 (R1.1-R1.4).\n          DiffTest fields: Name string (used as subtest name), Args []string (command-line args\n          passed to both binaries), Stdin []byte (nil means no stdin input -- EOF immediately),\n          Env []string (nil means inherit test process environment), ExitCode int (expected exit\n          code -- used only for documentation; actual comparison is between the two binaries),\n          Normalize []Normalize (slice of normalization functions applied in order; nil means no\n          normalization), WorkDir string (working directory for both binaries; empty means create\n          a per-test temporary directory), ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte (expected file content\n          after execution; nil means no file comparison). Define Normalize as func([]byte) []byte.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement binary execution per prd001-testutils R2 (R2.1-R2.5). Provide\n          RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) as the\n          primary API. For each DiffTest, execute goBinary and refBinary with identical Args,\n          Stdin, and Env. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code independently from each binary.\n          Impose a configurable timeout (default 10 seconds) on each invocation using\n          os/exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout. When WorkDir is empty, create a\n          per-test temporary directory via t.TempDir(). When WorkDir is set, use it as the\n          working directory for both binaries. Produce no output to the test process stdout or\n          stderr during a passing test run.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement comparison and divergence reporting per prd001-testutils R3 (R3.1-R3.6).\n          Apply each Normalize function in the DiffTest.Normalize slice (in order) to both\n          binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison. Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte;\n          any difference is a test failure. Compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte; any\n          difference is a test failure. Compare exit codes exactly; a difference is a test\n          failure. On divergence, report in the failure message: the DiffTest Args, the Stdin\n          content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), the reference binary stdout, the Go binary\n          stdout, the reference stderr, the Go stderr, and both exit codes. Run each DiffTest as\n          a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...) so individual test case failures are\n          identifiable in go test output.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement normalization hooks per prd001-testutils R4 (R4.1-R4.3). Support per-DiffTest\n          normalization via the Normalize slice field. Provide a built-in TimestampNormalizer\n          (a Normalize function) that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with the fixed\n          placeholder string \"TIMESTAMP\". Patterns to match: syslog format (\"Feb 19 12:34:56\" --\n          three-letter month, space-padded day, HH:MM:SS), ISO-8601 datetime\n          (\"2024-02-19T12:34:56\" with optional fractional seconds and timezone), and HH:MM:SS\n          elapsed time (\"00:05:32\"). Use regexp.ReplaceAll for pattern matching. Provide a\n          ComposeNormalizers(funcs ...Normalize) Normalize helper that returns a single Normalize\n          function applying each input function in order. When the input slice is empty, the\n          composed function returns its input unchanged.\n      - id: R5\n        text: |\n          Implement file-state comparison per prd001-testutils R5 (R5.1-R5.2). When\n          DiffTest.ExpectedFiles is non-nil, after both binaries exit, read each file path key\n          from the Go binary's working directory and compare its content byte-for-byte against the\n          expected []byte value. Report file content divergence in the same format as stdout\n          divergence, including the file path, expected content, and actual content.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Primary source file is difftest.go, named after the primary exported type DiffTest.\n          The package file must not be named testutils.go (project convention: file named after\n          primary exported type, not after the package).\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use os/exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout for binary invocation. Extract exit\n          code from exec.ExitError.ExitCode() on error; exit code 0 when err is nil. This avoids\n          platform-specific syscall.WaitStatus extraction.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          RunDiffTests calls t.Run(test.Name, func(t *testing.T) {...}) for each DiffTest to\n          produce named subtests per prd001-testutils R3.6. Each subtest is independent and can\n          be run individually via go test -run.\n      - id: D4\n        text: |\n          Normalizers live in a separate file (normalizers.go) to keep the primary difftest.go\n          focused on execution and comparison. TimestampNormalizer uses compiled regexp patterns\n          (package-level var via regexp.MustCompile) for performance.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: |\n          A cmd/ package can define a []DiffTest slice and call\n          RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests) from a TestXxx function with no other\n          setup. The function signature matches this exactly.\n      - id: AC2\n        text: |\n          A passing test run produces no output to stdout or stderr. A failing test prints the\n          triggering Args, Stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), both binaries' stdout, stderr, and\n          exit codes in the failure message via t.Errorf or t.Fatalf.\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          TimestampNormalizer replaces \"Feb 19 12:34:56\"-style timestamps, ISO-8601 datetime\n          strings, and HH:MM:SS elapsed strings with the placeholder \"TIMESTAMP\". Verified by\n          unit test in difftest_test.go.\n      - id: AC4\n        text: |\n          A DiffTest with ExpectedFiles correctly fails when the Go binary writes different\n          content than expected. Verified by unit test.\n      - id: AC5\n        text: |\n          A DiffTest that exceeds the 10-second timeout fails with a clear timeout message\n          rather than hanging the test run. Verified by unit test using a short timeout override.\n      - id: AC6\n        text: |\n          All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint. Verify by running\n          golangci-lint run ./pkg/testutils/... with no output.\n      - id: AC7\n        text: |\n          Unit tests in difftest_test.go pass via go test ./pkg/testutils/... and cover:\n          identical binary outputs (pass), divergent stdout (fail), divergent stderr (fail),\n          divergent exit code (fail), TimestampNormalizer replacement, ComposeNormalizers\n          chaining, and timeout expiration.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71597,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71597},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cfc4efcc-a511-46b2-8f82-2c74fbf1012c","uuid":"a944dbdc-bf24-43cb-b47e-ff1f1644ff6d"}
